    "decision": "Defer a standalone full [Q,K,512] gathered panel for sampled target throughput. For TTFT retain distinct sparse access, backing-page reuse and KV/attention tiling. A cache of a selected set has a new index generation at each producer; full backing pages from KV owner 20 can outlive those selections with correct publication and retirement.",
    "cost_model": "For one index generation with f consumers and U distinct physical KV rows, a dictionary pack in GM adds at least U reads and U writes of 1024 B per populated row before consumer access, if built separately from the producer. Consumer reads and row mapping remain. If produced on first use and retained in fast storage, at most (f-1)*U row fetches can be avoided in a one-backing-fetch-per-distinct-row-per-consumer model. Separate duplicated per-query panels Q*512*1024 from U*1024; measure reuse and actual memory traffic before converting to time.",
    "limits": [
      "Source and ordered native roles, not equal tensor addresses or identical generations proved by pointers. Page-table footprint is an upper bound on addressable slots. U is an upper bound for the scheduled CP partition, not observed selected membership or a per-query physical block map; total local query rows across the TP group equal scheduled tokens for every nonempty body. Prefix sharing, repeated indices, unused page entries and invalid output positions can reduce it.",
      "Weight footprint assumes ordinary full dense INT8 expert weights for each recorded nonempty expert. It rules out simultaneous residency of all touched weights and a retained pack where capacity is exceeded. Bypass, replacement and actual HBM/L2 transfers remain unknown.",
      "Each request is bound to its first nonempty schedule and own profile-client TTFT by the archived client slot, UUID events and layer history. The SMLA span is rank-local for the whole shared initiating batch; it is not exclusive request work or a bound on an end-to-end critical path across ranks or queued batches.",
      "Gathered KV does not eliminate layer-specific Q, SWA, masking, softmax or output; numerical order, distinct physical pages, request ownership, page lifetime, masks, indices and per-query lengths must be preserved."
    ],
